## Summary

The fix is complete. Here's what was done:

### Root Cause
The `Object.assign({}, defaults)` call in `_defu` function used the `__proto__` setter when the source object has `__proto__` as an own property (from JSON parsing), which changed the prototype of the target object instead of treating it as a regular property.

### Fix Applied
**File: `src/defu.ts`** - Replaced `Object.assign({}, defaults)` with a manual loop that explicitly skips `__proto__` and `constructor` keys:

```typescript
const object: Record<string, any> = {};
for (const key in defaults) {
  if (key === "__proto__" || key === "constructor") {
    continue;
  }
  object[key] = defaults[key];
}
```

This is consistent with the existing loop for `baseObject` which already had this protection.

### Test Added
Added a new test case in `tests/defu.test.ts` to verify the fix:

```typescript
test("does not pollute Object.prototype via __proto__", () => {
  const payload = JSON.parse('{"__proto__": {"polluted": true}}');
  defu({}, payload);
  defu(payload, {});
  defu(payload, payload);
  // @ts-ignore
  expect(({} as any).polluted).toBe(undefined);
});
```

### Verification
All 8 tests pass (including the new test).
